Dear 3Dnatives team,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 has been released, and there is one detail that may make it
unusually convenient for your editions: the application ships fully localised
in French, English, Spanish and German (plus Italian and Portuguese) -
interface, manual and example projects alike. Whichever edition picks the
story up, readers can follow it in their own language.

The core story is a five-minute test: import a downloaded model with the
wrong mounting hole, click the hole, and type a new diameter - without
finding the source CAD file or rebuilding the part. Solidon preserves the
original position and axis, including angled holes, and stores the change as
a reversible history step. From there, readers can add standards-based
heat-set inserts, screw seats or bearing seats and hand the plate straight
to their own slicer.

What sets it apart from both mesh editors and CAD suites:

* constrained sketching with pull-to-solid previews directly in the 3D view;
* reusable parametric components built from history steps, without code;
* an optional AI assistant that drives the same documented operations a
  click does - running locally via Ollama if preferred, never writing
  geometry itself;
* slicer hand-over with per-spool filament data carried into the right 3MF
  material slot, and the resulting G-code read back as a cross-check;
* Windows, macOS and Linux packages, no account, no cloud project store.

Download and overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide a before/after example (source STL, changed hole, printable
result) in any of the six languages, and the released build itself - the
complete demo runs free until 30 October 2026, so your editors can verify
every claim hands-on.

